.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_ALLOCATED_BYTES 3 "2026-09-16" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes \- report the memory a ranged pool holds for its entries
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes(ccol_r_mempool *rmp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns every tier's entry block added together, in bytes, which is what
a ranged pool costs.

Each tier is a pool in its own right with its own element size and
element count, and each one large enough to keep per-thread caches holds
a reserve of its own, so this exceeds the sum of the tier capacities.
It is the figure to use when sizing a ranged pool against a memory
budget, where working it out by hand is least practical.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp                       Ranged memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of every tier's entry block, summed
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The tiers' entry blocks are counted and nothing else, on the same terms
as ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(3): handles, per-thread caches and
entries served by a fallback policy are all outside this figure.

The value does not change over the pool's lifetime.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Acquires each tier's lock in turn, so it is a cold-path call and not
intended for use inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create (3)
